---
// Lesson 6.2a — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps
// isGrounded state via a ground layer raycast; the fair-flight contract.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>6.2a — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Yesterday's jump has a problem you already met in
    <a href="6.1a.html">6.1a</a>'s bug hunt: tap Space in mid-air and
    you fly. A jump you can spam is a jetpack. Today: one boolean,
    kept honest by physics — the ground check, the single most reused
    pattern in platformer code.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Here is the ground check the starter style calls for — new
    code, same voice as PlayerController (private fields, serialized
    knobs, Update work):</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private LayerMask groundLayer;
[SerializeField] private float groundCheckDistance = 0.2f;
private bool isGrounded;

private void Update()
{
    // Ask physics: is there ground just below my feet?
    RaycastHit2D hit = Physics2D.Raycast(
        transform.position,
        Vector2.down,
        groundCheckDistance,
        groundLayer);

    isGrounded = hit.collider != null;

    if (isPlatformer)
    {
        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space) &amp;&amp; isGrounded)
        {
            rb.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict: the raycast is a question you ask the physics engine
        60 times a second — "is a ground-layer collider within 0.2
        units under my center?" isGrounded is not stored anywhere
        else; it is <strong>re-derived every frame</strong>. Why is
        re-asking better than remembering (contrast with the lever's
        isOn, which remembers)? When is memory the right tool and when
        is asking?
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The jump condition is now a compound AND: key down AND
            grounded. In AP pseudocode that is
            <code>IF (key AND grounded)</code>. Why does the order of
            the checks not matter to the computer here — but matter
            enormously if the second check were expensive (a
            FindWithTag)?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the layer mask.</strong> Raycasts need a
    target. In the Platformer scene, select your tilemap/ground
    GameObjects, and in the Inspector set their Layer to a new layer
    named "Ground" (Add Layer... → type it, apply to all ground
    objects). Then drag "Ground" into the player's
    <code>groundLayer</code> field. Without the mask, the ray would
    hit the player's own collider — read that bug in Step 3.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — trust but verify with a debug ray.</strong>
    Gizmos make the invisible question visible:</p>
    <pre><code>private void OnDrawGizmos()
{
    Gizmos.color = Color.green;
    Gizmos.DrawLine(transform.position,
        transform.position + Vector3.down * groundCheckDistance);
}</code></pre>
    <p>Scene view: a green whisker under the character. On the
    ground it touches tile; in the air it dangles. If your jump does
    not work, the whisker is the FIRST thing you look at — does it
    actually reach the tile? (Character center to tile top might be
    longer than 0.2 — that is the number you tune.)</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the self-hit bug.</strong> Temporarily set
    groundLayer to "Everything". Now the ray hits the player's own
    collider: isGrounded is ALWAYS true and Space is a jetpack
    again. Layer masks are not decoration — they are how you say
    "I care about SOME objects." Same reason turret projectiles
    ignore the turret that fired them.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The flying ant</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set groundCheckDistance to 2. You can jump while
                standing on AIR near a ledge. Where does the
                character "stand" — and what did you just build
                (a hint from <a href="6.3a.html">6.3a</a> called
                coyote time, but broken)?</li>
            <li>Remove the groundLayer mask field entirely and pass
                ~0 (no mask). Which collider does the ray find
                first — the player's own — and what is the symptom?</li>
            <li>Jump onto a spike. Mid-hit, does isGrounded matter?
                Why does the death loop from
                <a href="2.2a.html">2.2a</a> never need to know
                about grounded-ness?</li>
            <li>Walk off a ledge and press Space exactly 0.1 seconds
                after leaving the ground. Rejected! Why is this the
                #1 player complaint about ground checks — and what
                does 6.3a promise to do about it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Check design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Two feet, one check: what would break if the ray fired
                from the character's center while the character
                stands on the very EDGE of a tile? Fix: fire it from
                the feet — which coordinate do you adjust?</li>
            <li>Raycast vs OnCollisionStay2D with ground tag — the
                other classic approach. One needs a mask and a
                distance; the other needs a layer/tag and gives you
                the ground object. Which is easier for moving
                platforms?</li>
            <li>Hard: the jump reads "key AND grounded" — but the
                animation should ALSO know (jump pose vs. walk
                cycle). Which parameter does your Animator gain, and
                which line sets it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build the raycast ground check: mask, distance, hit.collider != null</li>
            <li>Show it with a Gizmo whisker and tune the distance</li>
            <li>Explain the self-hit bug and what layer masks prevent</li>
            <li>Explain re-derived state vs remembered state, with an example of each</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — ground checks in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Physics2D.Raycast, LayerMask</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="6.3a.html">6.3a — Jump Feel: Fall Speed &amp; Coyote Time</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="6.2a" title="6.2a Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
